.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt GPART 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm gpart
.Nd show and change disks' partition tables
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm show
.Op Fl l
.Op Ar geom ...
.Nm
.Cm create
.Fl s Cm gpt | mbr
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm destroy
.Op Fl F
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm add
.Fl t Ar type
.Op Fl b Ar start
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl l Ar label
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm delete
.Fl i Ar index
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm resize
.Fl i Ar index
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm modify
.Fl i Ar index
.Op Fl l Ar label
.Op Fl t Ar type
.Ar geom
.Nm
.Cm set | unset
.Fl a Cm active
.Fl i Ar index
.Ar geom
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and writes the partition tables of disks: GPT, with its backup header
and entries at the disk's end, and MBR's four primary partitions.
A
.Ar geom
is a disk's name, such as
.Li ide0
or
.Pa /dev/sata0.2 ,
or a disk image file (512-byte sectors).
Partitions are named after their disk:
.Li ide0p2
on GPT,
.Li ide0s2
on MBR; a GPT partition's label also names it,
.Pa /dev/gpt/ Ns Ar label .
.Pp
Each change is written at once.
The kernel then reads the table again and makes the partitions'
.Pa /dev
nodes anew; while a partition of the disk is mounted or open, the old ones stay
until it is let go, and
.Nm
says so.
.Pp
A
.Ar start
or
.Ar size
is a number of sectors, or of bytes with a
.Cm k , m , g
or
.Cm t
suffix.
Sizes are rounded down to whole 4 KiB blocks.
.Pp
The verbs are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm show
Shows the tables of the named disks, or of every disk that has one: each
partition's first sector, size in sectors, index and type (with
.Fl l ,
its label), and the free space between them.
A GPT read from its backup, its primary header being damaged, is shown
.Li [CORRUPT] ;
any change writes both again.
.It Cm create
Writes an empty table:
.Cm gpt
or
.Cm mbr .
A disk with a table already must be
.Cm destroy Ns ed
first.
.It Cm destroy
Removes the table: the partition entries in sector 0 and, for GPT, both
headers.
Sector 0's boot code is kept.
A table with partitions is removed only with
.Fl F .
.It Cm add
Adds a partition of
.Ar type ,
numbered with the lowest free index.
It starts at
.Ar start ,
or else at the first 1 MiB boundary of the first free space it fits in, and is
.Ar size
long, or else as long as that free space.
.Fl l
gives a GPT partition its label.
.It Cm delete
Removes partition
.Ar index .
.It Cm resize
Makes partition
.Ar index
.Ar size
long, or as long as the free space after it allows; its start stays.
The file system on it is not resized.
.It Cm modify
Changes partition
.Ar index Ns 's
label or type.
.It Cm set , Cm unset
Sets or clears an MBR partition's
.Cm active
(boot) flag; setting it clears the others'.
.El
.Pp
The types are
.Cm oxfs ,
.Cm efi
(the EFI system partition),
.Cm bios-boot
(GPT only: where a BIOS boot loader's second stage goes),
.Cm swap ,
.Cm ms-basic-data
(FAT or NTFS),
.Cm linux-data
and
.Cm linux-swap ,
or any other as
.Cm \&! Ns Ar guid
or
.Cm \&!0x Ns Ar nn ,
the form
.Cm show
uses for a type it doesn't know.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 if the command did what was asked, 1 otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A disk for OxideBSD with UEFI and BIOS boot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpart create -s gpt ide1
gpart add -t bios-boot -s 64k ide1
gpart add -t efi -s 260m -l esp ide1
gpart add -t oxfs -l rootfs ide1
newfs_oxfs /dev/gpt/rootfs
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mkimg 1 ,
.Xr installer 8 ,
.Xr newfs_oxfs 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command first appeared in
.Fx 7.0 .
OxideBSD's takes FreeBSD's verbs and output; its tables are written by the
library
.Xr mkimg 1
and the installer use too.
